Falsifies `paging::domain::an_enumerated_page_lies_inside_the_grant`.

The defect: a grant with an unaligned base is accepted instead of refused. `grant_pages`'s own
doc argues this refusal at length and the argument is easy to read as belt-and-braces, since
`Mapper::map` would refuse the first page with `Misaligned` anyway. Losing it is an over-map:
the domain enumerates `base`, `base + 4096`, and so on, none of them page-aligned, and what the
IOMMU actually maps is the page each of those addresses falls inside, which starts BELOW `base`.
Bytes the device was never granted become device-reachable, which is the one direction this
confinement may never fail in.

Expected to fail: `assert!(page.is_multiple_of(PAGE_SIZE), "an unaligned page was mapped")`, for
any grant with an unaligned base and at least one page in it. The containment assertions after
it (`page >= r.base`, `end <= limit`) still hold under this defect, which is why the harness
needs the alignment assertion rather than containment alone.

The other guard in `grant_pages`, the wrap check, is broken by the `a_grant_the_domain_cannot_
express_is_refused` record instead, so the two patches are not copies.

diff --git a/crates/paging/src/domain.rs b/crates/paging/src/domain.rs
--- a/crates/paging/src/domain.rs
+++ b/crates/paging/src/domain.rs
@@ -44,9 +44,6 @@
 /// not mapped: the device reaches less than it was granted, never more. See notes/paging.md, "DMA
 /// domains".
 pub fn grant_pages(r: DmaRegion) -> Result<u64, MapError> {
-    if !r.base.is_multiple_of(PAGE_SIZE) {
-        return Err(MapError::Misaligned);
-    }
     if r.base.checked_add(r.size).is_none() {
         return Err(MapError::BadRegion);
     }
